  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nExiting of the upper level low has generally ended most\nprecipitation but the last gasp rain/snow mix showers (and\npockets of freezing drizzle in eastern Tucker County) is a\nresult of lift induced by a mid-level warm advection ahead of a\nshortwave ridge. Dry advection and high pressure behind the wave\nwill quickly end precipitation while allowing for some more\nclearing of clouds. Until then, due to risk for freezing\ndrizzle/rain with the loss of ice nuclei as moisture depth\nwanes, the Winter Weather Advisory for eastern Tucker County\nremains in effect until 9am. \n\nThe rest of the day will see limited warming before the\napproach of the next low pressure system continues the rising\ntemperature trend into the first half of Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nModels are in fairly strong agreement of a trough digging into\nthe Tennessee River Valley to start Sunday before it lifts\nslightly while exhibiting some negative tilt as it shuttles \neast. The surface low center ends up tracking west/northwest of\nthe region, promoting warm/moist advection through Sunday\nevening before the cold front passes near dawn Monday morning. \nThis favors rain showers spreading west to east Sunday afternoon\nand evening (which will produce the bulk of the roughly 0.5\" \ninch total accumulations) before showers linger into Monday \nmorning until cold front and upper trough axis finishes its \npassage. By Monday evening, surface high pressure will cement \nits place in the Upper Ohio River Valley region and favor a \nmulti-day period of dry weather with above normal temperature.\n\nThe biggest potential impact is another round of gusty winds as\nensemble means suggest a 40-50kt 850mb jet develops on the SE\nside of the low Sunday evening and positions roughly east of \nI-77 in OH, with lingering 35-40kt flow remaining over the\nregion Monday morning when lapse rates increase in the post-\nfrontal environment. REFS guidance is bullish on the Monday\nmorning period, suggesting 40-70% probabilities for lower\nelevations to see gusts in excess of 40 mph and 20-60% \nprobabilities for higher elevations to see gusts in excess of 50\nmph (which may necessitate Wind Advisory issuance). However, \nthis previous event showed a high bias around this area for the \nnewer REFS guidance, which remains the high-solution compared to\nother global models. So the current forecast will stick with \nthe NBM, which seems to balance the two model realms into a more\nlikely median value, but this bears monitoring for potential \nneed for highlights (again, mainly eastern Tucker County).",
